: perform accelerated gradient descent\n     **Final polished GD**: run high-tau gradient descent for refinement\n   - After each optimization round, check if any solution is valid (sum>=0.01) and if it improves objective\n   - Apply Fourier perturbation if stagnation occurs\n   - Take triangular steps back if the new objective is worse\n   Step 6: Print best progress each time a candidate improves\n\n   Helper functions use float32 internally and maintain gradient stability. The candidate state remains normalized but reversible.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Fixed length candidates: \n   - 10 * parent_size (high resolution)\n   - parent_size (medium)\n   - 5 * parent_size (low resolution)\n\n   Optimization rounds:\n   - Each length is optimized with:\n     * 500 seconds LP iteration\n     * 100 seconds Nesterov GD\n     * 50 seconds polished GD\n\n   Fallback strategies:\n   - If initialization fails, use uniform sequence: fill(0.002, length) (since 0.002*800<0.01)\n   - During optimization, clip values to 1000 and enforce non-negative\n\n   Decorators/Filters:\n   - Use @np.vectorize implementation of the closed-form process\n   - Prefer float32 for computationally intense operations (\u223c8.5% speed improvement)\n\n5. Budget Management:\n   Total available time: 1500 seconds\n   Breakdown:\n   - Around 450 seconds for high-resolution candidates (params initialized to 0.002)\n   - Around 600 seconds for medium-resolution (original parent)\n   - Remaining for low-resolution (\u223c450 seconds, with proper sum normalization)\n\n   We iterate via three rounds for each length and monitor for overresources convergence.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   All candidates are automatically normalized to positive sums if needed. We maintain a separate best candidate.\n\n   In the final return, we compute both:\n   - The candidate's normalized format (create a copy in standard float64)\n   - The original sequence without normalization (to maximize bit-level fidelity to the evaluator)\n\n   The best candidate is compared against all found candidates using the original evaluation function.\n\n   Note: The Fourier transforms are used to avoid numerical error accumulation during large convolutions.",
